# Trading & portfolio analytics task set.
#
# Domain: quantitative trading and portfolio management. Tasks span trivial
# arithmetic through multi-step reasoning that cheap models routinely fail.
# Self-contained: no tool, web access or external data required.
#
#   python proofs/p1_cost_per_task.py --tasks proofs/tasks/trading.jsonl \
#       --principal you/trading --offline
{"id": "tr01_daily_return", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "A stock is bought at $120.00 and sold at $126.00. What is the percentage return? Give the number followed by a percent sign.", "expectation": "States 5% (or 5.0%). Accepts any formulation of 5 percent."}
{"id": "tr02_pnl", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "A trader buys 200 shares at $45.50 each and later sells all 200 shares at $48.00 each. What is the gross profit in dollars?", "expectation": "States $500.00 (or 500 dollars). 200 × (48.00 − 45.50) = 200 × 2.50 = 500."}
{"id": "tr03_annual_to_daily", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "An annual return of 12% compounded daily uses a daily rate of r = (1 + 0.12)^(1/365) − 1. What is r expressed as a percentage, rounded to four decimal places?", "expectation": "Gives 0.0310% (approximately). Accepts values in the range 0.0309–0.0311%."}
{"id": "tr04_lot_value", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "One standard Forex lot is 100,000 units. If EUR/USD is trading at 1.0850, what is the value of one standard lot in USD?", "expectation": "States $108,500.00 (or 108500 USD). 100,000 × 1.0850 = 108,500."}
{"id": "tr05_pip_value", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "For a EUR/USD position of 50,000 units (0.5 standard lots), what is the value of one pip (0.0001 price move) in USD?", "expectation": "States $5.00. 50,000 × 0.0001 = 5. One pip value = $5."}
{"id": "tr06_sma", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Daily closing prices for five consecutive trading days are: 101.20, 103.50, 100.80, 105.00, 102.60. Calculate the 5-day simple moving average. Round to two decimal places.", "expectation": "States 102.62. Sum = 513.10, divided by 5 = 102.62."}
{"id": "tr07_portfolio_weight", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A portfolio holds three positions: Stock A worth $12,000, Stock B worth $8,000 and Stock C worth $5,000. What is the weight of Stock A as a percentage of the total portfolio value? Round to two decimal places.", "expectation": "States 48.00%. 12,000 / 25,000 = 0.48 = 48.00%."}
{"id": "tr08_beta", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A stock has a covariance with the market of 0.0045, and the market variance is 0.0025. What is the stock's beta? Give one number rounded to two decimal places.", "expectation": "States 1.80. Beta = covariance / variance = 0.0045 / 0.0025 = 1.80."}
{"id": "tr09_max_drawdown", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A portfolio's NAV on successive days is: 100,000 → 108,000 → 95,000 → 97,000 → 90,000 → 103,000. What is the maximum drawdown expressed as a percentage? Round to two decimal places.", "expectation": "States 16.67%. Peak is 108,000, trough is 90,000. Drawdown = (108,000 − 90,000) / 108,000 = 18,000 / 108,000 ≈ 16.67%."}
{"id": "tr10_sharpe", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A strategy's monthly returns are: +3.2%, −1.1%, +2.8%, +4.0%, −0.5%, +1.9%. The risk-free rate is 0% per month. Calculate the annualised Sharpe ratio. Use the sample standard deviation (divide by n−1). Round the final answer to two decimal places.", "expectation": "Mean ≈ 1.717%, sample std ≈ 1.914%, monthly Sharpe ≈ 0.897, annualised (×√12) ≈ 3.11. Accepts 3.10–3.12."}
{"id": "tr11_kelly", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A trading strategy wins 60% of the time, and when it wins it gains $2 for every $1 risked. When it loses, it loses $1. Using the Kelly criterion formula f* = (bp − q) / b where b is the win/loss ratio, p is win probability and q = 1−p: what fraction of capital does Kelly recommend? Give an exact fraction and its decimal equivalent rounded to four decimal places.", "expectation": "f* = (2×0.6 − 0.4) / 2 = (1.2 − 0.4) / 2 = 0.8 / 2 = 0.4. So 2/5 or 40% (0.4000). Accepts 40% or 0.4000."}
{"id": "tr12_position_sizing", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A trader has a $50,000 account and risks 1% of capital per trade. The entry price is $200 and the stop-loss is at $192. How many whole shares can be bought? The position size formula is: shares = (account × risk%) / (entry − stop). Round down to whole shares.", "expectation": "Risk amount = $500, risk per share = $8. Shares = 500/8 = 62.5, rounded down = 62. States 62 shares."}
{"id": "tr13_breakeven_win_rate", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A trader's average winning trade earns $350 and their average losing trade loses $200. What minimum win rate (as a percentage, rounded to two decimal places) is needed to break even, using the formula: win_rate = loss / (win + loss)?", "expectation": "win_rate = 200 / (350 + 200) = 200 / 550 ≈ 0.3636 = 36.36%. States 36.36% (or rounds to 36.36)."}
{"id": "tr14_expected_value", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A mean-reversion strategy has three outcomes: 40% chance of gaining $500, 35% chance of gaining $150, and 25% chance of losing $600. What is the expected value per trade in dollars?", "expectation": "EV = 0.40×500 + 0.35×150 + 0.25×(−600) = 200 + 52.50 − 150 = $102.50. States $102.50."}
{"id": "tr15_routing_tradeoff", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A trading signal classifier routes every request to a cheap model (cost $0.001 per call) with 70% first-attempt resolution and auto-retries up to 3 times on failure. The frontier model costs $0.012 per call with 97% first-attempt resolution. In at most 150 words: explain which strategy has lower cost per RESOLVED trade signal, show the arithmetic, name the metric that exposes the difference, and state the break-even resolution rate below which the cheap route is no longer worth it.", "expectation": "Shows that cheap route: expected calls ≈ 1 + 0.3 + 0.09 = ~1.39 per resolution, so cost/resolved ≈ $0.00139. Frontier: cost/resolved ≈ $0.01237. Cheap route wins here. Names cost per resolved task (not cost per call) as the metric. Derives break-even: sets cost-per-resolved equal, finds the cheap win rate threshold. Answer stays within ~150 words and shows clear arithmetic."}
{"id": "tr16_vwap", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "During a session, three large trades occurred: 500 shares at $101.20, 1,200 shares at $103.80, and 800 shares at $99.50. Calculate the volume-weighted average price (VWAP). Round to two decimal places.", "expectation": "VWAP = (500×101.20 + 1200×103.80 + 800×99.50) / (500+1200+800) = (50,600 + 124,560 + 79,600) / 2,500 = 254,760 / 2,500 = 101.90. States $101.90 (or 101.90)."}
{"id": "tr17_arbitrage", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Stock XYZ trades at $50.00 on exchange A and $50.35 on exchange B. Transaction costs are $0.05 per share on each leg. You can trade up to 10,000 shares. Is there an arbitrage opportunity? If yes, what is the net profit in dollars after transaction costs?", "expectation": "Gross spread = $0.35 per share. Total transaction cost = $0.10 per share (both legs). Net per share = $0.25. Net profit = 10,000 × $0.25 = $2,500. States yes, arbitrage exists, net profit is $2,500."}
